The incident regression (`test_stalled_slack_does_not_delay_popen`) is the meaningful red: the old code stalls 10.0s before `Popen` (two 5s notifier calls, `get_daily_thread_ts` then `notify_slack`) against a 1.5s budget — the exact shape of the 35s boot stall that overran the 30s ghost window.

`test_run_now_outside_boot_window_does_not_wait` is the one that passes before the change. It cannot fail on code that has no wait at all; it exists to pin the new wait's exclusion so a later change cannot make a human's run-now sit through 120 seconds. The occurrence assertion in it and in the boot-wait test cover acceptance point 4 — both assert the occurrence ends `success`, never `pending`.

